WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.670
Our minds have made an error.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:27.720
We look at these gliding beauties of marvelous
complexity and we think that they...and we...are

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.320
something of our own.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.430
That life is a kind of indescribable magic…

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:40.880
Even Darwin said, in his gorgeous conclusion
to On the Origin of Species, that life was

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:49.640
something that had been “originally breathed
into a few forms or into one.”

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:55.200
This completely understandable perspective
is nonetheless an error.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.530
There was no way for Darwin to know it...but
life….life is chemistry.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:08.240
From diatom to Diana, life is not a magical imbued trait, it is a process of the physics

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.860
of our universe.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:18.470
The precise and convoluted chemistry of life
requires specific physical and chemical situations.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:24.390
And this planet has a dizzying variety of
such circumstances that, over millions or

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:31.460
even billions of years, living chemical systems
have evolved to thrive in.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.869
But one thing that is always necessary is
energy.

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:40.960
A system without energy doesn’t just not
have life, it doesn’t have chemistry.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.740
And so, the story of life is the story of
captured energy.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:01.440
These are Oscillatoria a form of cyanobacteria
. Every second of every day, for billions

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:10.060
of years, the sun has been exploding, sending
energy to the earth in the form of sunlight.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.840
And on the other end— or really, on our
end—are photosynthetic bacteria like these

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:23.030
that use the energy of that light to construct
chemical complexity.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.370
These oscillatoria are gliding toward that
light so that they can harvest the energy

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:35.410
they need to produce a kind of chemical so
powerful...so important...that you will probably

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:39.310
be surprised to find out that it’s just
sugar.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:42.890
Oh sugar, such a chemical marvel.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:49.880
Just carbon and hydrogen and oxygen...but
an evolutionary innovation so fundamental

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:57.200
that it remains a primary source of nourishment
for even the most advanced living things.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:06.400
You may have seen this beautiful filamentous
algae called Spirogyra floating on the surface

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.690
of water, where it also takes advantage of
sunlight to produce its own food.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:19.819
Oscillatoria and Spirogyra are autotrophs...auto
for self...troph for nourishment.

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:26.510
In the world of ecology, we call these self-nourishers
the primary producers.

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:29.820
They are where it all begins.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.500
Primary Producers can turn the fundamental,
inorganic components of their surrounding

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.320
environment into food.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:44.150
In the case of photosynthetic (or, phototrophic)
organisms, the energy component of that surrounding

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.959
environment is light.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:51.240
There are also physical components...they
need carbon dioxide and water and nitrogen

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:57.020
and phosphorous for building materials, but
light is the energy.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:02.540
This is the first way organisms get food...they
make it.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.380
And most autotrophs use light...but not all!

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:11.280
There also lithotrophs, for example, that
use chemical bonds in minerals to thrive.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:20.239
Unfortunately, we don’t have any footage
of them...so just...more spirogyra for you!

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:26.599
And like plants in our macroscopic systems,
autotrophic microbes are the starting point

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:29.090
of the story of captured energy.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:37.410
Because, in their bid to hold onto and use
that energy, they themselves become balls

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.260
of stored energy.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:46.040
Not just in their energy storage systems like
their sugars, but their whole selves are bags

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:54.520
of entropically unstable complexity, ready
to fall into randomness the moment their chemistry

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.520
fails.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:01.770
I think we have to understand here that randomness
is the natural state of the universe.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:08.220
Not because of some inherent evil, but just
because there are literal trillions of random

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:14.600
ways for atoms and molecules to be scattered
about, and a vanishingly small number of ways

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.850
in which they will be organized.

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:24.580
And so, fighting randomness...that is the
cause of life, and that fight both requires

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:29.210
energy, but also, definitionally, stores energy.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:37.190
Because an organized state tends toward disorganization,
the path down that hill is one that releases

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.190
energy.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:41.710
And as that energy is released, it can be
captured.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:46.820
And if it can be captured by another organism,
it can be exploited.

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:51.910
And so we have our second system living organisms
use to stay alive.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:56.810
They don’t make their food...they eat it.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:04.040
Single-celled eukaryotes, also called protozoans,
dine on autotrophs, digesting their organic

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:09.490
components to supplement their own bodies
with the nutrients and energy they need to

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.810
grow and survive.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:18.100
And from there, nature weaves the complexity
of the food web, transferring nutrients and

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:23.360
energy through this network of organisms consuming
other organisms.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.870
Protozoans eat protozoans, and sometimes even
a few metazoans— the microscopic multicellular

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:34.270
organisms like rotifers, water bears, or fat
flatworms.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:41.070
And then you get to metazoans that eat protozoans,
like Daphnia, which in turn gets eaten by

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:47.610
fish, the microscopic now transitioning into
the macro.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:54.810
These eaters consolidate the energy, not just
keeping themselves alive, but feeding the

00:06:54.810 --> 00:07:01.080
more complex chemistry, pushing themselves
further and further from randomness...further

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:06.570
and further from the equilibrium state.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:12.840
But in the end, no matter what, life neither
big nor small lasts.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:18.060
Eventually, through random chance or wear
and tear, the chemical systems maintaining

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:25.000
this far-from-equilibrium state break, and
the living chemistry ends.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.169
And so we have our final opportunity for energy
transfer.

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:35.509
The last link in the stored energy chain,
the decomposers.

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:41.590
As microbes and plants and animals alike die,
they decompose into the environment, their

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:48.300
bodies broken down and recycled by organisms
like this Peranema, which is usually found

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.900
among decaying organic matter.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:56.990
Here, you can see its mouth, called the “rod
organ,” through which it takes in bits of

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:03.139
decomposing matter, and sometimes just other
organisms, and eats them, converting them

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:09.650
into carbohydrates that it stores in those
shiny paramylon grains.

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:15.570
Though these decomposers come at the end of
the food chain, well after the sunlight that

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:22.520
started it all, they are still surviving on
that stored energy originally flung out from

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:26.009
two atoms fusing in the core of the sun.

00:08:26.009 --> 00:08:31.229
And, of course, the role they play in our
ecosystems is still vital.

00:08:31.229 --> 00:08:37.361
As they break down once living organisms into
their chemical building blocks, they release

00:08:37.361 --> 00:08:43.709
those nutrients back into the environment,
ensuring that those building blocks are available

00:08:43.709 --> 00:08:49.200
to the autotrophs that will begin the cycle
all over again.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:57.560
As we delve into more and more ecosystems,
the vast number of factors that have shaped

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:01.220
a microbe’s evolution become more and more
apparent.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:08.589
But one thing remains consistent, the chain
of stored energy...the primary producers...the

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:15.769
eaters...and the decomposers, sustaining the
chemical system that keeps them complex...the

00:09:15.769 --> 00:09:21.360
energy that holds them together, and that
is holding you together right now, preventing

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:27.660
you from simply falling into randomness.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:33.840
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:37.120
and thank you, also, to all of these wonderful people

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:42.440
for supporting this channel on Patreon and making it possible for us to do this.

00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:46.180
If you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James Weiss

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:49.240
check out Jam and Germs on instagram.

